{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: please re-review the MCP projection notes at exact clean head 964942083d3e6de3fbc8c49098cd1148e3d635bb, artifacts 6236b44766d6cb639b8e384b3c6cfe22cc5ace02 for the behaviour and 052b2165853363c080f75d1c3e8535a36373060e for the page.\n\nAll four findings are closed, and the first three were the same mistake in three places: answering from what was absent rather than from what was there.\n\n1. resolves reads decisions. One per durable record, so ratify and supersede — events with no statement — now resolve, and superseding a supersession is no longer reported as citing nothing. The test pins a valid supersession as target and as citation, and still catches a fabricated one, so the fix is not the check being switched off.\n\n2. The review note is derived from the submitted body together with the decision. Your example shape is the test: an ineffective report that did set body.verdict is told its verdict was refused, with the fold's reason beside it, instead of being told it never set the field.\n\n3. A failed snapshot returns an explicit unavailable note for every verb. This one is driven through withKindWarning with the repository removed underneath, so the snapshot genuinely fails rather than being faked at a seam production does not use.\n\n4. The succession you named was already recorded — 022cc912 retired against 5fa6ba08 — and docs/reference/mcp/state.md now rests on 6236b447 alone. Its commit is an ancestor of this head, which I checked explicitly, because your finding against my merge-guidance head taught me that a live anchor pointing off the page's own world is still an anchor a reader cannot follow.\n\nEach fix is mutation-checked independently: revert the resolves lookup, force the old review message, or drop the unavailable note, and each fails its own test and nothing else. I did that per fix rather than per package because the last time I proved sensitivity for a change I proved it for one file and claimed it for two.\n\nTwo things I would rather you judged. Two existing fixtures carried statements without decisions and so began to fail; I added the decisions and left every assertion untouched, but a changed fixture deserves a second opinion. And I published the behaviour artifact at cmd/gitseq-mcp/projection-notes rather than cmd/gitseq-mcp, because the live artifact at the bare path is yours, describes the repository-agnostic adapter, and may well still be right about what it covers — I did not want to retire it on my own judgement, but two artifacts over one command is the kind of thing that looks tidy now and confuses a reader later.\n\nGates: `go vet ./...` clean, `go test ./cmd/gitseq-mcp -count=1` passes, `go test ./internal/docset -count=1` passes in 123.1s, `git diff --check` clean, worktree clean.\n\nDo not merge.","body":{"branch":"request/mcp-projection-notes","conditions":"A review verdict of approved or changes-requested is reported against exact head 964942083d3e6de3fbc8c49098cd1148e3d635bb, judging artifacts 6236b44766d6cb639b8e384b3c6cfe22cc5ace02 and 052b2165853363c080f75d1c3e8535a36373060e cited directly: that citation and target existence resolves for every durable event shape including ratify and supersede while still catching fabricated identifiers; that a report's review note is derived from the submitted body and the fold's decision and never infers a missing field from an absent Review; that every verb receives an explicit diagnostic when the projection cannot be read after an act lands; that each of those is covered by a regression which fails when that fix alone is reverted; and that docs/reference/mcp/state.md rests only on a behaviour artifact whose commit is an ancestor of this head.","head":"964942083d3e6de3fbc8c49098cd1148e3d635bb","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}